---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import SectionHeader from "../components/SectionHeader.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import RepCard from "../components/RepCard.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
	title="Lead From The Hip — leadership development that measures behavior"
	description="Assessment-led leadership coaching that turns your dispositions into specific routines, then measures whether they stuck. For anyone who leads a team."
>
	<Hero
		eyebrow="Development · assessment · coaching"
		standfirst="Knowledge is cheap. Behavior is everything. Success requires getting in more reps at doing the right things until they become habit. That's the game: Pick the two or three behaviors that actually cost you, then practice them relentlessly. Not until you get them right — until you can't get them wrong."
		primaryCta={{ label: "See how it works →", href: "#method" }}
		secondaryCta={{ label: "Work with me", href: "/work-with-me" }}
	>
		Less knowing,<br />
		<span class="accent">more doing.</span>
	</Hero>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="01"
			title="Knowing isn't doing."
			lede="You have probably already done at least one of these. All three stop at knowing. None of them build the daily habit of doing."
		/>
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-8">
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The book with the secret sauce.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-sm">
					Five pillars. Ten habits. One framework, guaranteed to work for every leader
					who reads it — as though a weekly one-to-one costs a naturally organized
					person and a naturally spontaneous one the same amount. It doesn't. There is
					no secret sauce, and pretending otherwise is why the resolution lasted nine
					days.
				</p>
			</Card>
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The class that ended in applause.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-sm">
					Ninety minutes, a good facilitator, a room that nodded along together. The
					applause at the end was real. By Monday, everyone ran the same meeting the
					same way they always had.
				</p>
			</Card>
			<Card>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-2">The report that named you accurately.</h3>
				<p class="text-muted text-sm">
					It was well-built and it was right about you. You nodded. It stung a little.
					Then it went in a drawer, because a description of who you are is not an
					instruction for what to do on Thursday.
				</p>
			</Card>
		</div>
		<p class="pull-quote measure text-ink text-xl">
			Every one of those stops at knowing. What builds the daily habit of doing is
			smaller than any of them — one question, asked again, until it changes something.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="method" class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="02"
			title="Build your own feedback loop."
			lede="Most people never find out how they're actually coming across. This is how you build that feedback loop yourself — ask one question of them, ask one question of yourself, and repeat until something changes."
		/>

		<h3 class="text-xl mb-3">One piece of advice</h3>
		<div class="bg-surface border-[1.5px] border-petrol rounded-[3px] p-6 flex flex-col gap-5 mb-6">
			<p class="eyebrow text-petrol">Ask someone you work with</p>
			<p class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-medium text-ink measure">
				"If you could give me one piece of advice that would make me more effective, what
				would it be?"
			</p>
			<div class="grid grid-cols-[3.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 gap-y-1 border-t border-rule pt-4">
				<span class="eyebrow text-brass">Then</span>
				<p class="text-ink font-medium">"What have you seen that makes you say that?"</p>
				<span></span>
				<p class="text-muted text-sm measure">
					Advice alone isn't actionable — "be more decisive" tells you nothing about
					Monday. The second question turns their advice back into the thing they
					actually saw, which is the part you can work on.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="mb-8">
			<p class="eyebrow text-muted mb-3">How to run it</p>
			<div class="flex flex-col">
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">01</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-sm">
						<span class="font-medium">Don't schedule it.</span> This is a hallway
						question, not a meeting. The moment it needs an invite, it stops happening.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">02</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-sm">
						<span class="font-medium">Say nothing back but thank you.</span> No
						explaining, no context, no "well, the reason I did that." Defending it once
						teaches them not to answer next time.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">03</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-sm">
						<span class="font-medium">Ask five people, not one.</span> One answer is an
						opinion. The same answer from three people is your reputation.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-[2.5rem_1fr] gap-x-3 py-3 border-t border-b border-rule">
					<span class="font-mono text-sm text-muted tabular-nums">04</span>
					<p class="text-ink text-sm">
						<span class="font-medium">Ask again in ninety days.</span> If the advice has
						changed, you have. If it hasn't, you haven't.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="mb-8">
			<h3 class="text-xl mb-1">Where the real learning happens</h3>
			<p class="text-muted text-sm mb-4 measure">
				Most people wait for an annual review to learn how they're doing. This builds
				your own feedback loop instead — pay attention daily, then convert the pattern
				into an intention weekly.
			</p>
			<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
				<div class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-5">
					<div class="flex justify-between eyebrow text-brass mb-2">
						<span>Every night</span><span>2 min</span>
					</div>
					<p class="text-ink text-sm">
						What happened today that I'm still thinking about — and what does it tell
						me about how I'm leading?
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="bg-surface border border-rule rounded-[3px] p-5 flex flex-col gap-3">
					<div class="flex justify-between eyebrow text-brass">
						<span>Every Friday</span><span>5 min</span>
					</div>
					<p class="text-ink text-sm">
						What theme keeps showing up in my journal this week — and what is it
						telling me?
					</p>
					<p class="text-ink text-sm">
						What is the one thing I'll do differently next week — and when exactly
						will I do it?
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="text-muted text-sm mt-4 measure">
				Miss a day — just resume, don't reconstruct it. Nothing to write about — that's
				information too. Give it six weeks before you expect to see a pattern, three
				months before you judge whether it worked.
			</p>
		</div>

		<p class="pull-quote measure text-ink text-lg">
			One conversation won't change much. Neither will one week of writing. The gap
			between how you see yourself and how others describe you when you're not in the
			room only closes with repetition.
		</p>

		<div class="bg-surface border-[1.5px] border-petrol rounded-[3px] p-6 mt-8">
			<p class="eyebrow text-petrol mb-2">Not yet</p>
			<p class="text-ink measure">
				Do this daily and weekly for six months before you go looking for what's next.
				Section 03 is the one or two percent — the kind of edge a professional athlete
				chases once the fundamentals are already automatic, one specific rep at a time.
				Chase it too early and it's just another idea you never actually run.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="03"
			title="What a rep actually looks like"
			lede="The loop will take most leaders a long way. Occasionally, though, one specific behavior won't move. A rep isolates that exact moment and drills a specific alternative until it's automatic — the same way an athlete trains one move, not the whole game."
		/>
		<RepCard />
		<p class="text-muted text-sm mt-4 measure">
			No new personality required. Just the same specific move, run the same way,
			until it holds up under pressure.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader number="04" title="Who this is for" lede="Two honest lists." />
		<div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-3">This works if you:</h3>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink flex flex-col gap-2 text-sm">
					<li>Have tried to change a habit before and watched it not stick</li>
					<li>Care about how you come across to people, not just what you meant to do</li>
					<li>
						Are willing to practice something small and specific, on purpose, even
						when it's inconvenient
					</li>
					<li>Would rather find out whether it worked than feel good about having tried</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div>
				<h3 class="text-lg mb-3">This is not for you if you:</h3>
				<ul class="list-disc list-inside text-ink flex flex-col gap-2 text-sm">
					<li>Want a label more than a behavior to change</li>
					<li>Are looking for a diagnosis of someone else, not yourself</li>
					<li>Want a program to roll out to a team or an organization</li>
					<li>
						Need clinical support — that is a different profession and I will say so
						and refer
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="05"
			title="This is what doing it looks like."
			lede="Most weeks, I'll post how the loop actually went for me — what a colleague said, what showed up in the journal, what I changed because of it. Eventually, other people's versions too. Reading about it isn't the work. Watching someone else actually do it is the next best thing."
		/>
		<a
			href="https://substack.com/@leadfromthehip"
			class="inline-block bg-petrol text-paper px-5 py-3 rounded-[3px] font-medium hover:opacity-90 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-petrol"
		>
			Follow the loop →
		</a>
	</section>

	<section class="max-w-[1080px] mx-auto px-6 py-12 border-t border-rule">
		<SectionHeader
			number="06"
			title="If you want more"
			lede="Most of what's here is free — the method, the examples, the Substack. From time to time I take on a handful of clients who want the full version: an assessment, a written plan, and the ninety-day proof that it worked."
		/>
		<a
			href="/work-with-me"
			class="inline-block bg-petrol text-paper px-5 py-3 rounded-[3px] font-medium hover:opacity-90 focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-petrol"
		>
			Work with me →
		</a>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
